  <h2 id="secret">Internal Test Secret</h2>
  <p>The <code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code> env var gates the <code>X-Route-Target</code> and <code>X-Risk-Score</code> response headers. When set, headers only emitted for requests with matching <code>X-Internal-Test-Auth</code> header. When unset, headers never emitted (fail closed). This allows scenario scripts to verify routing decisions without exposing oracles to real users or blind pentests.</p>

  <h2 id="running">Running Tests</h2>
  <pre><code># Lua unit tests (plain Lua interpreter, no OpenResty needed)
cd ids/reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua && lua tests/test_&lt;module&gt;.lua

# Dashboard tests
cd dashboard && python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

# Scenario tests
bash testing/scenario_*.sh</code></pre>

  <h2 id="results">Validation Results</h2>
  <p>Two of the dashboard's report generators turn a live stack into evidence: the <strong>Segmentation
  Validation</strong> run proves per-session isolation, and the <strong>Exploit test-scenario</strong> run
  measures detection against the seeded CVEs. Both export a PDF with per-request scores, routing decisions,
  resource footprints and statistics. The figures below come from representative recorded runs; regenerate
  them from the dashboard to validate a given deployment.</p>

  <h3>Segmentation validation — verdict EXCLUSIVE on both layers</h3>
  <p>The run opens ten independent browser windows, each with its own cookie jar, User-Agent and language, so
  the proxy sees ten separate users. Each one attacks, is flagged, and is expected to land on a honeypot pool
  <em>of its own</em>. The run also injects random session tampering — cookie clearings plus hijack and
  forgery attempts — to confirm a window cannot be pushed onto, or steal, another window's pool.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Recorded run</th><th>Honeypot layer</th><th>Windows</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
    <tr><td>2026-10-24</td><td>WordPress (reverse-proxy) level</td><td>10, each on its own pool</td><td>EXCLUSIVE</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2026-10-18</td><td>Database-proxy level</td><td>10, each on its own pool</td><td>EXCLUSIVE</td></tr>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No shared pools below the resource ceiling.</strong> Every flagged session received an exclusive pool; round-robin reuse only happens once scaling hits <code>POOL_MAX</code> / the memory and CPU budget. In the 2026-10-24 run the limit (10 of <code>POOL_MAX</code>=10) was reached at 2:33, yet <strong>zero</strong> windows ended up sharing a pool — every later diversion still found a free spare.</li>
    <li><strong>Tampering was rejected.</strong> Forged and hijacked session cookies were scored as tampering and refused; a window that cleared its cookies was re-recognised by the passive fingerprint rather than handed a clean slate. The run injected 5 cookie clearings (all 5 kept their session) and 2 forgeries — 1 browser-storage forgery <em>ignored</em> (the server never reads <code>localStorage</code>/<code>sessionStorage</code>) and 1 cookie hijack <em>rejected</em> (<code>bad_signature</code>, +50 scored as tampering, logged in Redis <code>session_cookie_rejections</code>). The real cookie is <code>SERVERID = &lt;session id&gt;.&lt;HMAC&gt;</code> and only the proxy holds the key, so a forged one cannot be signed.</li>
    <li><strong>Reassignment after scale-down worked.</strong> After a forced idle scale-down to the three Compose-declared pools, all 7 windows whose pool was removed were reassigned to a surviving pool — HTTP 200, same session, no 502 from the gone container and no fall-through to production. Carts stayed intact except where a window had cleared its own cookies (expected: the cart lives only in the <code>fh_cart</code> cookie, so the server keeps no copy to restore).</li>
    <li><strong>Both <a href="/docs/engine/layers">deception layers</a> pass.</strong> The database-proxy run exercises only data-layer patterns and checks the shared filesystem at the end (see <code>ids/db_proxy/COVERAGE.md</code>), since it cannot isolate file or upload attacks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Scaling and latency (measured, 2026-10-24 WordPress-proxy run)</h4>
  <p>The run opened 10 windows (3 of them attacking only after pools were pre-built for them) and took
  5:12, plus 0:31 of clean-up after the reassignment check. <code>pool_manager</code> adds one honeypot
  pool — an eshop container plus its own database seeded from production — per session that finds no free
  pool, up to the resource limit; the figures come from its own event log and from the windows themselves
  (routing read after every step and every 2&nbsp;s while waiting).</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Resource ceiling</td><td>12,288&nbsp;MB, 12 CPUs, <code>POOL_MAX</code>=10 (each pool reserves 768&nbsp;MB / 0.75 CPU in the budget)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pools built during the run</td><td>7 (on top of 3 Compose-declared) — build time median 27&nbsp;s, max 40&nbsp;s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Build phases (per pool)</td><td>copy template dominates (10–27&nbsp;s); database start ~3&nbsp;s; rotate password ~1&nbsp;s; content sync 1–2&nbsp;s; WordPress start ~6&nbsp;s; then create/register</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Borrow while scaling (case A)</td><td>4 windows borrowed a ready pool round-robin, then moved to their own after a median 34&nbsp;s (max 43&nbsp;s)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Share at the ceiling (case B)</td><td>0 windows — the limit was hit at 2:33 but a free spare was always available</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Failed page loads</td><td>16 first-attempt failures, every one <code>ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED</code> while Docker was adding/removing a pool's network interface; all retried with back-off and loaded in the end</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Pool memory footprint.</strong> The 10 pool containers peaked at a measured
  <strong>4,687&nbsp;MB (~469&nbsp;MB per pool)</strong> — well under both the 768&nbsp;MB budget charge
  (deliberate headroom) and the 1,536&nbsp;MB container ceiling (1,024 eshop + 512 database). The host as a
  whole used 13,191&nbsp;MB of 24,032&nbsp;MB, because it also runs production, Redis, Suricata and the rest.</p>
  <p><strong>Page-load latency</strong> (the same product page, one window at a time, timed navigation→load
  finished) was effectively indistinguishable between production and a honeypot pool:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Served by</th><th>Median</th><th>p90</th><th>Max</th><th>n</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Production</td><td>360&nbsp;ms</td><td>382&nbsp;ms</td><td>384&nbsp;ms</td><td>12</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Own honeypot pool</td><td>372&nbsp;ms</td><td>469&nbsp;ms</td><td>495&nbsp;ms</td><td>16</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The probe loads the same product page three times during the run (all windows on production, after the
  scale-up, at the end), so step loads made while ten windows render at once do not distort the comparison.</p>
  <p><strong>Forced scale-down.</strong> With the idle timeout
  (<code>POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS</code>=1800&nbsp;s) too long for a test, the run forced the scale-down:
  requested at 4:36, finished in 16.2&nbsp;s, dropping from 10 pools to 3 (the Compose-declared 1, 2, 3 are
  only freed, never removed). Pool-container memory fell 4,687&nbsp;MB&nbsp;→&nbsp;1,515&nbsp;MB
  (−3,172&nbsp;MB) and running containers 20&nbsp;→&nbsp;6.</p>

  <h4>Scaling and latency (measured, 2026-10-18 database-proxy run)</h4>
  <p>On the <a href="/docs/engine/layers">database layer</a> a pool is only a honeypot database cloned from
  production and scrubbed; the one shared shop connects a diverted session to it on every request. Pools are
  therefore much cheaper and faster to add. The run had 10 windows (3 delayed) and took 4:47, plus 0:22 of
  clean-up.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Pools built during the run</td><td>9 (on top of 1 Compose-declared) — build time median 15&nbsp;s, max 17&nbsp;s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Build phases (per pool)</td><td>database start 6–9&nbsp;s; clone + scrub 7–9&nbsp;s; query log and create/register under 1&nbsp;s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Borrow while scaling (case A)</td><td>6 windows borrowed a pool, then moved to their own after a median 9&nbsp;s (max 11&nbsp;s)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Share at the ceiling (case B)</td><td>1 window shared a pool after the limit (10 of <code>POOL_MAX</code>=10) was reached at 2:27; every window still ended on a pool of its own</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pre-build for 3 delayed windows</td><td>3 free pools after 29&nbsp;s (2 built for them, 1 spare already free)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool memory</td><td>peak 2,133&nbsp;MB across 10 pools (~213&nbsp;MB per pool, against a 512&nbsp;MB database limit and a 768&nbsp;MB budget charge)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Page-load latency</td><td>production median 362&nbsp;ms (p90 405, n=13); own pool 320&nbsp;ms (p90 370, n=17)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Forced scale-down</td><td>10&nbsp;→&nbsp;1 pools in 10.2&nbsp;s (each removal ~0.3&nbsp;s); pool memory 2,133&nbsp;→&nbsp;218&nbsp;MB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Failed page loads</td><td>40 first-attempt failures, all while a pool was built or removed; every one loaded on retry</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Because every pool's PHP runs in <code>production_eshop</code> on this layer, the diverted sessions' CPU
  load shows on production's container, not on the pool containers.</p>
  <p>How these reports were made trustworthy — thirteen rounds of audits against <code>pool_manager</code>'s
  event log and the fixes they led to — is described in <a href="/docs/testing/validation-rounds">Validation
  Rounds</a>.</p>

  <h3>Exploit test-scenario — detection rate per attack class</h3>
  <p>Each seeded CVE is replayed from a freshly reset threat state (Redis and the proxy's shared dicts
  flushed) so its score is that exploit's own isolated value, read back from the diagnostic
  <code>X-Risk-Score</code> / <code>X-Route-Target</code> headers. Rates below are from a recorded
  WordPress-proxy-level run and use Wilson confidence intervals.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Attack class</th><th>Diverted to honeypot (any request)</th><th>Caught on the first request</th></tr>
    <tr><td>CVE exploits</td><td>13 / 14 (93%)</td><td>13 / 14</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Plugin / theme targets</td><td>14 / 16 (88%)</td><td>14 / 16</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Generic attack patterns</td><td>15 / 18 (83%)</td><td>7 / 18 (rest on request 2–3)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WordPress-core probes</td><td>14 / 16 (88%)</td><td>6 / 16 (rest on request 2–3)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Backend-facing</td><td>26 / 29 (90%)</td><td>18 / 29</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">No honeypot bypasses once routed</div>
    <p>On the WordPress-proxy level the exploit report recorded zero honeypot bypasses: a diverted request is
    served by a separate decoy eshop with its own files, PHP runtime and database, so every exploit is
    contained after routing. Lower-confidence attacks (generic and user-enumeration probes) sometimes need a
    second or third request before the accumulated score crosses the threshold — repetition tips them over.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lifecycle">Service Start/Stop Reports</h2>
  <p>Starting or stopping a stack from the Workloads page records a <strong>services report</strong>: host
  CPU/RAM from <code>/proc</code> and per-container CPU/memory from the cgroup files every 0.25&nbsp;s, container
  states from <code>docker compose ps</code> (~1.5&nbsp;s) and Docker events, numbered event markers, Global
  Visualization screenshots before, during and after, and a Warnings section listing failed or unstarted
  containers, error lines logged during the recording, killed containers and missing host settings.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Recording</th><th>Window</th><th>Result</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge start, 2026-10-17</td><td>58&nbsp;s</td><td>all containers healthy at 19.2&nbsp;s, every one-shot job exit 0, host CPU peak 35&nbsp;%, no warnings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge stop, 2026-10-17</td><td>8&nbsp;s</td><td>stack gone by 5.8&nbsp;s, <code>down</code> done at 8.2&nbsp;s, no container killed (24&nbsp;s with five SIGKILLs before <code>init: true</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge stop, database layer, 2026-10-18</td><td>8&nbsp;s</td><td>no warnings, nothing killed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SIEM start, 2026-10-18</td><td>70&nbsp;s</td><td>all healthy at ~58&nbsp;s, host CPU peak 82&nbsp;% at 56.8&nbsp;s while Kibana starts, no warnings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge start, 2026-10-18</td><td>64&nbsp;s</td><td>healthy, one warning: <code>vector_outbound</code>'s first healthcheck was refused because the SIEM was still starting</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>A tile's red badge on Global Visualization counts every line with an error word since the dashboard's log
  tail started (including 50 lines of replayed scrollback); the report's Warnings count only lines logged
  during the recording, so the two numbers can differ.</p>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Pure/adapter split enables 23 unit test files. Comprehensive coverage of all pure modules. Dashboard validation catches NDJSON drift. Segmentation and exploit runs turn a live stack into reproducible, statistics-backed evidence on both deception layers.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> No end-to-end integration tests. Blind pentest requires manual setup. No CI/CD integration.</p>
